1
00:00:03,461 --> 00:00:06,757
On the small rooftop garden,
the wind blows again today.

2
00:00:06,762 --> 00:00:09,315
The tomatoes have not turned red yet.

3
00:00:09,711 --> 00:00:12,635
Well then, let's begin today's story.

4
00:00:17,674 --> 00:00:21,267
Ta-da! All twelve seedlings sprouted!

5
00:00:21,658 --> 00:00:23,749
Wow! All of them? For real?

6
00:00:23,807 --> 00:00:25,935
Yes! Not one is missing!

7
00:00:25,935 --> 00:00:27,374
And every single one is healthy!

8
00:00:27,455 --> 00:00:30,343
You can't tell if they're healthy yet.

9
00:00:30,833 --> 00:00:33,141
I can! You can tell just by looking!

10
00:00:33,141 --> 00:00:35,281
Then that one, third from the left.

11
00:00:35,342 --> 00:00:38,491
This one? She's fine, look.

12
00:00:58,021 --> 00:01:00,357
She's lying flat, Tomoe. Completely flat.

13
00:01:01,897 --> 00:01:03,003
She's just resting!

14
00:01:03,003 --> 00:01:05,092
Plants need breaks too, you know!

15
00:01:05,165 --> 00:01:07,387
Plants don't nap, not normally.

16
00:01:07,439 --> 00:01:10,234
Hmm, she's just a bit of an individualist!

17
00:01:10,492 --> 00:01:12,850
Tomoe, you've named them too?

18
00:01:12,917 --> 00:01:16,779
Of course! From the left,
Tomako, Tomata, Tomami!

19
00:01:16,779 --> 00:01:18,266
They're all tomatoes, you know.

20
00:01:18,878 --> 00:01:22,685
Names matter more! Oh,
this one's Nana-chan!

21
00:01:23,154 --> 00:01:24,826
The one lying down is Nana-chan?

22
00:01:24,826 --> 00:01:29,139
Yes! She's lying down, thinking
things over. She'll get up, I'm sure!

23
00:01:29,163 --> 00:01:31,538
She's not thinking. She's collapsed.

24
00:01:31,691 --> 00:01:33,850
I can't hear you! I can't hear you!

25
00:01:33,962 --> 00:01:35,973
Riku, that's a bit harsh...

26
00:01:36,047 --> 00:01:38,475
Say it gently and she'll fall over again.

27
00:01:38,574 --> 00:01:39,333
Nana-chan, please wake up.

28
00:01:39,333 --> 00:01:40,632
You're doing great, okay?

29
00:03:18,778 --> 00:03:20,251
Haru, come here for a second.

30
00:03:20,255 --> 00:03:21,228
What?

31
00:03:21,228 --> 00:03:24,147
Look at this leaf through the magnifier.

32
00:03:26,531 --> 00:03:28,704
There are tiny green
bugs on the underside.

33
00:03:28,777 --> 00:03:29,802
Bugs? Wait, really?

34
00:03:30,337 --> 00:03:31,710
I think they're aphids.

35
00:03:31,788 --> 00:03:32,748
How many?

36
00:03:32,773 --> 00:03:34,051
One, two, three... seventeen.

37
00:03:34,051 --> 00:03:35,434
That's just on this one leaf.

38
00:03:35,434 --> 00:03:37,865
Seventeen on this one leaf alone?

39
00:03:49,930 --> 00:03:53,116
Technically, there are more. I
only counted the ones I can see.

40
00:03:53,861 --> 00:03:54,749
No way...

41
00:03:54,870 --> 00:03:59,289
They multiply fast. One can
turn into dozens in a week.

42
00:04:02,429 --> 00:04:05,111
Wait, could it spread to all the pots?

43
00:04:06,863 --> 00:04:10,610
Yes, the wind carries them, and
they move leaf to leaf too.

44
00:04:14,016 --> 00:04:16,479
That means every plant on the roof.

45
00:04:16,592 --> 00:04:17,824
Every one...

46
00:04:17,911 --> 00:04:19,965
The tomatoes, Nana-chan, all of them.

47
00:04:20,059 --> 00:04:22,155
And Nana-chan's already lying down.

48
00:04:22,369 --> 00:04:23,342
Not the time for jokes.

49
00:04:23,388 --> 00:04:24,586
I'm not joking.

50
00:04:24,961 --> 00:04:28,565
Let's check every pot right
now, starting at the edge.

51
00:04:29,790 --> 00:04:32,196
One by one, check under every leaf.

52
00:04:33,803 --> 00:04:34,769
Got it.

53
00:04:34,830 --> 00:04:36,305
So how do we get rid of them?

54
00:04:36,341 --> 00:04:38,673
I think a soap spray should wash them off.

55
00:04:38,792 --> 00:04:41,171
But if we use anything stronger,
we'll need the principal's okay—

56
00:04:43,576 --> 00:04:44,874
Do we have to tell Mitsuhashi?

57
00:04:44,874 --> 00:04:46,363
He's going to find out anyway.

58
00:04:46,385 --> 00:04:47,770
Hiding it isn't right,
Haru. Let's be honest.

59
00:04:47,770 --> 00:04:49,085
We don't have enough data yet.

60
00:04:49,085 --> 00:04:51,046
Let me count everything again.

61
00:04:51,294 --> 00:04:52,192
I'll recount it all.

62
00:04:52,246 --> 00:04:55,931
I'll only bring numbers to the
principal, then we decide.

63
00:04:55,970 --> 00:04:57,503
...Fine.

64
00:05:01,063 --> 00:05:03,250
Do what you want. But
I'm watching you do it.

65
00:05:06,338 --> 00:05:09,873
Principal, you actually
came all the way up here?

66
00:05:10,209 --> 00:05:13,846
Checking progress is part of my
job. Don't look so surprised.

67
00:05:29,618 --> 00:05:32,942
Haa... seventy-two steps. Someone
please install a railing.

68
00:05:33,294 --> 00:05:35,767
The railing is still
waiting on budget approval.

69
00:05:35,865 --> 00:05:36,537
I didn't ask for excuses.

70
00:05:36,537 --> 00:05:37,652
Hand me the schedule. In writing.

71
00:05:37,756 --> 00:05:38,756
Schedule?

72
00:05:38,756 --> 00:05:40,015
In writing.

73
00:05:40,131 --> 00:05:42,059
We have one! Hang on!

74
00:05:42,159 --> 00:05:43,404
Here you go.

75
00:05:44,508 --> 00:05:50,049
1. Water every morning at seven. 2.
Check leaves. 3. Grafting practice.

76
00:05:50,049 --> 00:05:51,524
I'll read it from the top.

77
00:05:51,524 --> 00:05:55,245
Watering, observation,
grafting, pest control.

78
00:05:55,838 --> 00:05:58,769
Pest control only started
yesterday, hasn't it?

79
00:05:59,369 --> 00:06:00,600
Yes.

80
00:06:00,654 --> 00:06:02,301
...And the harvest?

81
00:06:02,345 --> 00:06:03,393
There's no harvest date on here.

82
00:06:03,393 --> 00:06:05,002
Not one before the festival.

83
00:06:05,781 --> 00:06:08,776
That... we haven't settled that yet.

84
00:06:08,923 --> 00:06:11,145
"Not decided" isn't an
answer I can accept.

85
00:06:11,464 --> 00:06:17,089
Technically, it's at least forty
days from flowering to ripe fruit.

86
00:06:17,292 --> 00:06:19,357
Forty days. How many
days until the festival?

87
00:06:19,379 --> 00:06:20,984
Twenty-three days.

88
00:06:23,282 --> 00:06:25,068
The math doesn't work, does it?

89
00:06:28,307 --> 00:06:29,268
Plants can't read deadlines, sir.

90
00:06:29,268 --> 00:06:30,226
Blame the tomatoes, not us.

91
00:06:30,347 --> 00:06:32,004
Owada, watch your mouth.

92
00:06:32,004 --> 00:06:34,151
I'm just stating facts.

93
00:06:34,248 --> 00:06:35,928
...Which one is the tomato?

94
00:06:35,974 --> 00:06:38,113
The far left one, by the stake!

95
00:06:38,482 --> 00:06:40,561
Her name's Robin! She's doing great!

96
00:06:40,575 --> 00:06:42,151
Only the name is doing great.

97
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:45,700
Its leaves are curling. It should
be getting enough water, though.

98
00:06:45,954 --> 00:06:48,353
Recheck how much water it gets.

99
00:06:49,063 --> 00:06:49,999
Yes!

100
00:06:50,103 --> 00:06:53,539
Principal, is that tomato your favorite?

101
00:06:56,461 --> 00:06:57,613
Not particularly.

102
00:07:00,117 --> 00:07:01,331
It just has a nice shape.

103
00:07:01,432 --> 00:07:03,616
...Well, I'm heading back down.

104
00:07:06,679 --> 00:07:09,166
Today we're grafting tomatoes.

105
00:07:12,182 --> 00:07:13,256
Grafting?

106
00:07:14,834 --> 00:07:16,926
We're joining two stems together.

107
00:07:17,014 --> 00:07:19,266
Wait, so they become one plant?

108
00:07:19,344 --> 00:07:20,385
Pretty much.

109
00:07:20,413 --> 00:07:21,851
Where's the tape?

110
00:07:23,594 --> 00:07:26,653
Sensei, isn't that just a bandage?

111
00:07:26,744 --> 00:07:28,103
Close enough.

112
00:07:28,103 --> 00:07:31,207
Cut the ends at an angle,
because the angle matters.

113
00:07:31,292 --> 00:07:32,277
Like this?

114
00:07:32,289 --> 00:07:35,282
Minori, what's the angle?

115
00:07:36,013 --> 00:07:38,875
Technically, somewhere
around thirty degrees.

116
00:07:38,950 --> 00:07:39,992
You measured it?

117
00:07:40,310 --> 00:07:41,222
By eye.

118
00:07:41,284 --> 00:07:42,612
Is it right?

119
00:07:43,037 --> 00:07:43,934
Yeah.

120
00:07:43,934 --> 00:07:46,932
Then you line them up and
wrap them tight with tape.

121
00:07:47,658 --> 00:07:50,563
Wrap too tight and you'll choke the stem.

122
00:07:50,664 --> 00:07:51,830
Gently, but so they don't come apart.

123
00:07:51,830 --> 00:07:52,784
Like a good friendship.

124
00:07:52,792 --> 00:07:55,950
Sensei, stop saying philosophical things.

125
00:07:55,950 --> 00:07:57,879
I've never said anything philosophical.

126
00:07:57,928 --> 00:08:03,253
Sometimes the root is
stronger than the fruit.

127
00:08:03,352 --> 00:08:04,764
The root over the fruit?

128
00:08:04,904 --> 00:08:07,902
The fruit's just what the root made.

129
00:08:09,845 --> 00:08:11,274
Huh.

130
00:08:12,314 --> 00:08:14,460
Hey, where did you learn that tape trick?

131
00:08:14,555 --> 00:08:17,138
From the melon bread
stall guy by the station.

132
00:08:17,138 --> 00:08:18,187
Wait, the melon bread guy?

133
00:08:18,285 --> 00:08:19,106
He taught me grafting.

134
00:08:19,106 --> 00:08:20,606
Also, the bread's really good.

135
00:08:20,656 --> 00:08:21,792
Grafting at a food stall?

136
00:08:21,792 --> 00:08:24,740
On slow days, he'd fiddle
with branches all afternoon.

137
00:08:25,277 --> 00:08:29,353
Sensei, that's obviously a lie.

138
00:08:29,470 --> 00:08:30,212
It's not a lie.

139
00:08:30,212 --> 00:08:32,519
The melon bread is the real deal.

140
00:08:35,819 --> 00:08:37,982
You've been staring at
that pot for a while.

141
00:08:40,391 --> 00:08:41,474
Nothing.

142
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:43,291
Liar. You've been at it since earlier.

143
00:08:43,364 --> 00:08:46,350
...I grew a tomato with my grandpa once.

144
00:08:46,407 --> 00:08:48,304
First year of middle school, that spring.

145
00:08:48,435 --> 00:08:53,574
We watered it every morning and evening,
and we set the stakes up together.

146
00:08:53,574 --> 00:08:54,851
Mm.

147
00:09:18,392 --> 00:09:21,871
Then the fruit came in and started
turning red, and then a typhoon hit.

148
00:09:23,452 --> 00:09:26,025
Grandpa was in hospital,
so I was alone with it.

149
00:09:26,115 --> 00:09:28,396
By morning, the plant was
snapped flat, stakes and all.

150
00:09:28,451 --> 00:09:29,496
...I see.

151
00:09:29,566 --> 00:09:31,967
Every tomato was lying in the dirt.

152
00:09:31,986 --> 00:09:34,985
I picked up what I could
and threw out the rest.

153
00:09:37,179 --> 00:09:39,889
I haven't come back to the club since.

154
00:09:40,006 --> 00:09:40,743
Owada...

155
00:09:40,758 --> 00:09:42,903
Don't feel sorry for me.

156
00:09:43,491 --> 00:09:43,791
I'm not.

157
00:09:43,791 --> 00:09:45,155
I just keep thinking about
how that must have felt.

158
00:09:45,210 --> 00:09:46,481
I'm sorry.

159
00:09:46,554 --> 00:09:49,252
So you think one failure means
you're finished for good?

160
00:09:49,685 --> 00:09:52,131
No, that's not what I meant.

161
00:09:52,623 --> 00:09:54,909
Then what did you mean?

162
00:09:58,064 --> 00:09:58,948
That it was my fault.

163
00:09:58,948 --> 00:09:59,997
I should have been there.

164
00:10:00,040 --> 00:10:03,597
It wasn't your fault. A storm
doesn't ask permission.

165
00:10:03,684 --> 00:10:06,375
But I tied the stakes. Badly.

166
00:10:06,486 --> 00:10:08,294
Maybe the knots were loose.

167
00:10:08,312 --> 00:10:12,161
One failure doesn't make you a failure.

168
00:10:13,227 --> 00:10:17,219
I've killed three plants myself.
Please don't tell anyone.

169
00:10:17,336 --> 00:10:18,309
Yeah, right.

170
00:10:18,336 --> 00:10:21,044
Really. And one of them
is almost dead right now.

171
00:10:24,646 --> 00:10:25,434
That's why we're saving it.

172
00:10:25,434 --> 00:10:26,340
Nobody gets left behind.

173
00:10:26,353 --> 00:10:28,416
...You think it's not too late?

174
00:10:28,466 --> 00:10:31,013
We'll make it. Help me.

175
00:10:37,689 --> 00:10:40,036
We should tell the
principal about the aphids.

176
00:10:40,149 --> 00:10:42,515
Why? Keeping quiet won't do any good.

177
00:10:42,571 --> 00:10:44,585
If we tell him, they'll take the roof.

178
00:10:44,585 --> 00:10:46,126
Even so, lying is not okay.

179
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:48,171
It's not a lie. We just
haven't said it yet.

180
00:10:48,186 --> 00:10:49,156
It's the same thing.

181
00:10:49,156 --> 00:10:50,245
Huh?

182
00:10:50,367 --> 00:10:53,009
Give me three days and
I'll get every last one.

183
00:10:53,353 --> 00:10:56,117
If it's fixed in three
days, why not say so first?

184
00:10:57,595 --> 00:11:00,196
When we report, it'll be easier
if they're already gone.

185
00:11:00,230 --> 00:11:02,987
What if they spread
while we're hiding them?

186
00:11:03,081 --> 00:11:04,830
They won't. I've already
checked every leaf.

187
00:11:04,830 --> 00:11:06,538
Only the bottom ones are affected.

188
00:11:06,538 --> 00:11:07,409
Really?

189
00:11:07,492 --> 00:11:10,158
Yeah. Minori checked them too.

190
00:11:10,353 --> 00:11:11,479
Did she count them?

191
00:11:33,610 --> 00:11:35,883
She did. About two hundred.

192
00:11:35,957 --> 00:11:38,610
Two hundred?! That's not
a small number at all.

193
00:11:38,660 --> 00:11:40,617
Get rid of them and it
becomes a small number.

194
00:11:41,052 --> 00:11:42,060
But...

195
00:11:42,158 --> 00:11:43,622
I don't want to hide things.

196
00:11:43,622 --> 00:11:45,173
Let's just tell the truth.

197
00:11:45,173 --> 00:11:47,558
Getting yelled at, I can live with.

198
00:11:48,223 --> 00:11:50,282
But if we lose the roof,
we can't do anything.

199
00:11:50,282 --> 00:11:53,729
That's why we fix it first, then report.

200
00:11:53,736 --> 00:11:55,480
Isn't the order backwards?

201
00:11:55,742 --> 00:12:00,206
Backwards is fine. If the results
are there, he can't complain.

202
00:12:00,247 --> 00:12:01,391
Hmm...

203
00:12:04,322 --> 00:12:04,877
He won't.

204
00:12:04,877 --> 00:12:06,073
I'll make sure of it.

205
00:12:06,081 --> 00:12:07,232
Are you sure?

206
00:12:07,268 --> 00:12:09,181
...No. But I'll try.

207
00:12:09,207 --> 00:12:11,574
Being too honest can be a problem too.

208
00:12:11,603 --> 00:12:12,799
I think about it every day.

209
00:12:12,799 --> 00:12:14,407
...Fine, your way.

210
00:12:14,407 --> 00:12:16,806
But only three days.

211
00:12:31,590 --> 00:12:34,030
Spray every leaf, top and bottom.

212
00:12:35,689 --> 00:12:38,719
The soap's diluted a hundred
times. I measured it.

213
00:12:38,742 --> 00:12:40,299
Whee! Shh, shh!

214
00:12:41,978 --> 00:12:44,042
Too much. You'll drown the leaves.

215
00:12:44,113 --> 00:12:46,429
These guys get a shower too!

216
00:12:46,469 --> 00:12:49,850
Don't forget the undersides.
That's where the aphids hide.

217
00:12:49,901 --> 00:12:50,825
We'll do the undersides too.

218
00:12:50,825 --> 00:12:51,572
Everyone take a pot.

219
00:12:51,681 --> 00:12:52,792
Got it.

220
00:12:54,759 --> 00:12:57,536
This one's Tomakichi! Take that, splash!

221
00:13:00,750 --> 00:13:02,125
Don't name them.

222
00:13:02,644 --> 00:13:04,203
Too late, I already did.

223
00:13:04,289 --> 00:13:10,218
Statistically, named pots survive longer.
I haven't actually measured that.

224
00:13:10,479 --> 00:13:12,135
Then Tomakichi is strong!

225
00:13:12,135 --> 00:13:13,304
Hands moving, please.

226
00:13:13,331 --> 00:13:14,501
Yes!

227
00:13:15,579 --> 00:13:17,680
Sorry! My hand slipped!

228
00:13:18,836 --> 00:13:20,216
Eek! My eyes!

229
00:13:20,342 --> 00:13:22,079
Ha ha, it stings! This is
kind of funny, though.

230
00:13:22,124 --> 00:13:24,188
Are you okay? I'm really sorry.

231
00:13:24,203 --> 00:13:26,261
I'm fine! Tastes like soap!

232
00:13:26,261 --> 00:13:30,815
A little won't hurt.
Rinse it off to be safe.

233
00:13:31,220 --> 00:13:32,119
Rub, rub.

234
00:13:32,130 --> 00:13:34,707
Don't rub your eyes with those
gloves. They're covered in dirt.

235
00:13:34,810 --> 00:13:35,711
Dirt? Wait, really?

236
00:13:35,711 --> 00:13:36,630
Dirt, and whatever the bugs left behind.

237
00:13:36,630 --> 00:13:37,377
Congratulations, you've got both.

238
00:13:37,414 --> 00:13:38,776
Aaah!

239
00:13:41,247 --> 00:13:42,471
I'll go wash them!

240
00:13:42,700 --> 00:13:43,703
Hands and face. Maybe the heart too.

241
00:13:43,703 --> 00:13:45,306
Everything gets dirty eventually.

242
00:13:46,507 --> 00:13:48,368
Sensei, could you help a little?

243
00:13:48,427 --> 00:13:49,377
I'll try. Preferably
after the melon bread.

244
00:13:49,384 --> 00:13:52,076
Three days, right? Start counting.

245
00:13:59,519 --> 00:14:01,292
The festival's only ten days away.

246
00:14:01,292 --> 00:14:02,963
That's way too early.

247
00:14:05,081 --> 00:14:08,626
It's not too early. Bugs like that
can be cleared in three days.

248
00:14:08,626 --> 00:14:12,518
Technically, that's wishful
thinking, not a plan.

249
00:14:12,583 --> 00:14:14,659
It's not wishful. It's a plan.

250
00:14:15,248 --> 00:14:16,785
Then show me the numbers.

251
00:14:16,785 --> 00:14:17,530
Real ones.

252
00:14:17,609 --> 00:14:18,454
Sure, sure.

253
00:14:18,530 --> 00:14:22,232
Diluted soap, sprayed three
times, every other day.

254
00:14:22,244 --> 00:14:23,697
How many bugs are we talking about?

255
00:14:23,697 --> 00:14:24,704
Didn't count.

256
00:14:24,762 --> 00:14:25,652
What?

257
00:14:25,773 --> 00:14:29,268
Counting takes longer than
fixing. I checked every leaf.

258
00:14:29,673 --> 00:14:32,252
Shouldn't you assess the
damage before you treat it?

259
00:14:32,374 --> 00:14:34,770
I checked the undersides of every leaf.

260
00:14:35,836 --> 00:14:36,826
Every single one?

261
00:14:36,826 --> 00:14:37,917
Mostly.

262
00:14:38,005 --> 00:14:39,261
Mostly isn't good enough.

263
00:14:39,261 --> 00:14:40,337
Where's the record?

264
00:14:40,381 --> 00:14:41,316
In my head.

265
00:14:41,330 --> 00:14:42,447
Terrible.

266
00:14:44,848 --> 00:14:45,358
Trust me.

267
00:14:45,358 --> 00:14:46,502
I know these plants.

268
00:14:46,550 --> 00:14:48,165
Trust comes after data.

269
00:14:48,165 --> 00:14:49,927
You're a real pain, you know that?

270
00:14:49,942 --> 00:14:53,623
Someone has to count, or
you'll get sloppy, Owada.

271
00:14:57,259 --> 00:14:59,664
Coming from you, that stings.

272
00:15:01,351 --> 00:15:03,524
A sting means I hit the mark.

273
00:15:04,133 --> 00:15:07,371
The principal inspects on
festival day. We can't wait.

274
00:15:07,371 --> 00:15:08,680
That's exactly why it's too early.

275
00:15:08,765 --> 00:15:11,004
Three days. I'll clear the bugs.

276
00:15:11,165 --> 00:15:14,942
Only if they're really gone
in three days will I wait.

277
00:15:15,015 --> 00:15:15,964
Deal.

278
00:15:15,997 --> 00:15:17,070
I'll be counting.

279
00:15:20,009 --> 00:15:22,050
I'll come check in three days.

280
00:15:29,379 --> 00:15:33,096
Hey, Sensei! Why do tomatoes die?

281
00:15:33,527 --> 00:15:35,347
That came out of nowhere.

282
00:15:35,797 --> 00:15:38,662
Because Mini-Taro is lying
down and won't get up!

283
00:15:40,164 --> 00:15:41,772
He's not sleeping, you know.

284
00:15:43,979 --> 00:15:45,568
Eh? So he's not going to wake up?

285
00:15:45,624 --> 00:15:48,553
Whether he wakes up depends on you, maybe.

286
00:15:50,086 --> 00:15:52,382
Me? Then I'll water him!

287
00:15:52,416 --> 00:15:54,458
Too much water isn't good for him either.

288
00:15:54,458 --> 00:15:55,997
Then what am I supposed to do!

289
00:15:56,015 --> 00:15:58,322
Wait. Then watch.

290
00:15:58,854 --> 00:16:00,486
I'm bad at waiting!

291
00:16:01,189 --> 00:16:01,989
I know.

292
00:16:01,989 --> 00:16:04,584
That's cheating, Sensei!
That's not an answer!

293
00:16:05,187 --> 00:16:07,535
Plants know the answer better than I do.

294
00:16:07,538 --> 00:16:09,406
Plants don't talk!

295
00:16:09,527 --> 00:16:10,309
Mm...

296
00:16:10,354 --> 00:16:13,299
But I still want to know why they die!

297
00:16:13,342 --> 00:16:15,456
Plants are stubborn.

298
00:16:15,811 --> 00:16:17,088
Stubborn?

299
00:16:19,317 --> 00:16:20,317
Yes, stubborn.

300
00:16:20,420 --> 00:16:23,080
Once they've made up their
minds, they don't bend easily.

301
00:16:23,114 --> 00:16:24,577
People are just as stubborn.

302
00:16:24,577 --> 00:16:25,653
Maybe even more.

303
00:16:25,763 --> 00:16:27,194
Are you stubborn too, Sensei?

304
00:16:27,212 --> 00:16:28,792
I'm only stubborn about being sleepy.

305
00:16:28,792 --> 00:16:29,467
Ask anyone.

306
00:16:29,471 --> 00:16:31,369
Hehe, that's so you, Sensei!

307
00:16:31,457 --> 00:16:32,348
I see...

308
00:16:32,384 --> 00:16:34,688
So Mini-Taro is still fighting?

309
00:16:34,802 --> 00:16:36,873
Yes. The roots are still working.

310
00:16:36,911 --> 00:16:37,852
Somehow that makes me feel better.

311
00:16:37,852 --> 00:16:38,992
Like someone agreed with me.

312
00:17:00,692 --> 00:17:03,381
Strange. We're talking about
dying, and I still feel better.

313
00:17:03,970 --> 00:17:08,069
Being stubborn is good. It
means they want to live.

314
00:17:15,289 --> 00:17:17,286
Shh. You're too loud.

315
00:17:17,694 --> 00:17:19,889
You talked first.

316
00:17:22,905 --> 00:17:24,046
I did not.

317
00:17:24,336 --> 00:17:25,886
You did. You said "hey."

318
00:17:26,321 --> 00:17:27,632
That was a greeting.

319
00:17:30,166 --> 00:17:32,779
Nobody greets people on stairs.

320
00:17:33,523 --> 00:17:34,758
I do.

321
00:17:38,273 --> 00:17:39,969
Fine. So what now?

322
00:17:39,969 --> 00:17:42,694
Do we tell the principal or not?

323
00:17:42,777 --> 00:17:43,721
No.

324
00:17:43,721 --> 00:17:44,818
I hate lying.

325
00:17:44,859 --> 00:17:45,567
It's not lying.

326
00:17:45,567 --> 00:17:47,389
We just haven't said it yet.

327
00:17:50,743 --> 00:17:52,755
What are you two discussing on the stairs?

328
00:17:52,755 --> 00:17:53,721
Huh! Nothing!

329
00:17:53,808 --> 00:17:54,773
The weather.

330
00:17:54,797 --> 00:17:56,304
It's sunny today.

331
00:17:56,771 --> 00:17:57,621
We were talking about sunshine.

332
00:17:57,720 --> 00:17:59,001
And rain.

333
00:18:02,197 --> 00:18:03,170
Could you not?

334
00:18:03,170 --> 00:18:04,549
Whispering on a landing.

335
00:18:04,549 --> 00:18:06,014
Classic signs of a secret.

336
00:18:06,132 --> 00:18:07,197
We're not hiding anything.

337
00:18:07,511 --> 00:18:09,690
If you're hiding nothing,
what can't you tell me?

338
00:18:09,716 --> 00:18:10,941
That's...

339
00:18:11,468 --> 00:18:12,720
Tomatoes!

340
00:18:13,064 --> 00:18:14,413
Idiot.

341
00:18:15,955 --> 00:18:17,041
Tomatoes.

342
00:18:17,041 --> 00:18:18,409
We're growing them. Really well.

343
00:18:18,490 --> 00:18:20,426
Your report is due by Monday.

344
00:18:20,426 --> 00:18:21,345
We'll submit it.

345
00:18:21,345 --> 00:18:22,978
I'll let this one go.

346
00:18:26,006 --> 00:18:29,304
The plants on that roof
fall under school rules.

347
00:18:29,304 --> 00:18:30,252
Don't forget.

348
00:18:30,332 --> 00:18:31,281
Yes.

349
00:18:31,393 --> 00:18:34,193
...Grow the tomatoes properly.

350
00:18:39,439 --> 00:18:40,698
Okay, turning the light on.

351
00:18:40,811 --> 00:18:42,869
Wow, the leaves are glowing!

352
00:18:43,989 --> 00:18:45,998
They're not glowing.
Technically, it's reflection.

353
00:18:46,087 --> 00:18:47,654
What's reflection?

354
00:18:47,693 --> 00:18:49,333
When light bounces back off something.

355
00:18:49,354 --> 00:18:49,872
Any bugs?

356
00:18:49,872 --> 00:18:51,250
Keep your eyes open.

357
00:18:51,280 --> 00:18:52,625
Not right now.

358
00:18:52,666 --> 00:18:53,623
Phew, good!

359
00:18:53,639 --> 00:18:56,505
We take turns on watch.
Nobody pushes themselves.

360
00:18:56,613 --> 00:18:57,798
I'll go first!

361
00:19:01,037 --> 00:19:02,172
Just ten minutes.

362
00:19:02,225 --> 00:19:04,286
In ten minutes, how
many bugs can you find?

363
00:19:04,989 --> 00:19:07,098
Don't make a racket on the roof
in the middle of the night.

364
00:19:07,760 --> 00:19:08,844
Riku-kun!

365
00:19:08,943 --> 00:19:11,060
I wasn't planning on coming.

366
00:19:11,060 --> 00:19:12,327
But you came.

367
00:19:13,631 --> 00:19:14,896
I just wanted to check the light.

368
00:19:14,896 --> 00:19:15,877
Not the plants, obviously.

369
00:19:15,969 --> 00:19:17,758
That's a bad lie.

370
00:19:18,221 --> 00:19:19,350
Shut up.

371
00:19:19,378 --> 00:19:20,474
Show me the graft.

372
00:19:20,524 --> 00:19:22,247
The tape's a little loose.

373
00:19:22,337 --> 00:19:23,658
I'll retape it.

374
00:19:26,018 --> 00:19:28,072
Is it okay to touch it?

375
00:19:31,564 --> 00:19:35,073
Wait, look here. The cut's still alive.

376
00:19:35,825 --> 00:19:36,735
Really?

377
00:19:36,853 --> 00:19:38,338
The roots are winning.

378
00:19:38,418 --> 00:19:42,508
So you remember what
Miyashita-sensei said.

379
00:19:42,586 --> 00:19:43,738
I can't seem to forget it.

380
00:19:43,747 --> 00:19:45,567
Do you want to go back, then?

381
00:19:45,645 --> 00:19:47,347
Go back to the old way?
Honestly, I don't know.

382
00:19:47,347 --> 00:19:49,224
It's not as simple as it used to be.

383
00:19:50,959 --> 00:19:52,746
Why not do both?

384
00:21:32,102 --> 00:21:35,066
The principal's coming to inspect
before the festival, apparently.

385
00:21:35,152 --> 00:21:36,326
Wait, this week?

386
00:21:36,448 --> 00:21:37,579
This week.

387
00:21:37,651 --> 00:21:40,743
You promised the bugs would be
gone in three days, remember?

388
00:21:40,795 --> 00:21:42,085
That was your promise, not mine.

389
00:21:42,145 --> 00:21:46,277
On inspection day, someone with mud on
their face gets caught by the principal.

390
00:21:46,388 --> 00:21:47,287
Yeah, yeah.

391
00:21:47,287 --> 00:21:47,996
Don't run off, Riku.

392
00:21:47,996 --> 00:21:49,197
I need you up here for this.

393
00:21:49,248 --> 00:21:51,393
Continued on the rooftop, next time.

